//! Error: an `= <body>` function body must be a string literal
//! Tags: parser, inline, silent-fallback

// `= <expr>` parsed the expression, THREW IT AWAY, and kept an empty inline
// source -- so `fn c(x: int) -> int = x + 1` emitted `function c(x) return end`
// and answered nil. The comment called it recovery from a malformed inline
// body, but it ran on the ordinary path and produced a silently wrong program.
//
// Rock has no expression-bodied function form. If one is ever wanted, it is a
// language decision with a lowering behind it, not a parser fallthrough.
// Reproduces identically on `operator` and on the `Type.method` form.

//=== TEST CASE: expression_bodied_fn ===
fn c(x: int) -> int = x + 1

fn main() {
    let _ = c(1)
}
